  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =6(2\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
